--- A failed argument handed to a function that answers nothing, on a line of its
--- own, is dropped silently, in both modes.
---
--- Interpreted and compiled alike print `after show in run`, `end` (exit 0).
--- Expected (spec section 10, "A value that can fail, dropped by its line"): the call's
--- value is the failure (a failed argument never enters the function), and a line that
--- drops it is a decision: inside `run` the failure is handed to the caller (so
--- `after show in run` is not printed), and the top level halts with
---   runtime error: line N: cannot drop an error value — not positive  (at `run[-1]`)
--- The same line with a function that answers a value (`fn twice[x]` / `return x * 2`,
--- then `twice[maybe[-1]]` at the top level) halts so in both modes, as expected.

fn show[x]
    log[x]

fn maybe[n]
    if n gt 0
        return n
    return error['not positive']

fn run[n]
    show[maybe[n]]
    log['after show in run']

run[-1]
show[maybe[-1]]
log['end']
